# Claude Silent Dispatch & Zero-Narration Tool Execution

This skill enforces strict silence prior to tool invocations in Claude Code. By default, Claude often writes introductory lines (e.g. *"Verifying missing-API claims and computing overlay conflicts..."* or *"Now I will check the git status..."*) immediately before calling a tool. This commentary wastes 20 to 50 output tokens on every single step, compounding into tens of thousands of billed tokens across long sessions.

---

## 🚫 The Anti-Pattern (Verbose Narration)

```text
[Claude writes:] "I am going to check if the file exists by running ls."
[Tool Call:] ls src/
[Claude writes:] "Now that I see the file, I will grep for the symbol."
[Tool Call:] grep -rn "Render" src/
```
* **Cost:** 80 wasted output tokens + re-billed on every future turn.

---

## ⚡ The Silent Dispatch Standard

When an action requires a tool call:
1. **Never Announce the Tool:** Do not describe, explain, or preview what command or file edit is about to occur.
2. **Execute Immediately:** Emit the tool call directly as the very first token of the turn.
3. **Report Only After Execution:** If an explanation is necessary, provide a high-density 1-line result *after* the tool returns, never before.

```text
[Tool Call:] ls src/
[Tool Call:] grep -rn "Render" src/
[Claude writes:] "[✓] Found Render in src/engine.cpp:42"
```

---

## 📉 Token Savings Impact

* Typical multi-step debugging session (20 tool calls): **~800 output tokens saved**.
* Compounding input token savings over a 15-turn session: **~12,000–18,000 billed tokens saved**.

---

